The problem is here:
```
	switch (args.size()) {
		case 1: tn->vtype = Variant::INT; break;
		case 2: tn->vtype = Variant::VECTOR2; break;
		case 3: tn->vtype = Variant::VECTOR3; break;
	}
```
For the sake of optimization gdscript stores arguments for 'for' loops in Vector: Vector2 for 2 arguments and Vector3 for 3 respectively. Vector in Godot stores float values (to increase performance) so we have this problem here.

Turning off this optimization vanishes the bug:
```
	if (op->op == OperatorNode::OP_CALL && op->arguments[0]->type == Node::TYPE_BUILT_IN_FUNCTION && static_cast<BuiltInFunctionNode *>(op->arguments[0])->function == GDScriptFunctions::GEN_RANGE) {
		//iterating a range, so see if range() can be optimized without allocating memory, by replacing it by vectors (which can work as iterable too!)
	/* ... */
```
The solution for constant int 'range' arguments would be something like also checking (static_cast\<int\>(0xFF000000) \< value) && (value \< static_cast\<int\>(0x01000000)
Though this code for ConstantNodes is never executed, guess it's another minor bug:
```
	bool constant = false; // should be true

	for (int i = 1; i < op->arguments.size(); i++) {
		args.push_back(op->arguments[i]);
		if (constant && op->arguments[i]->type == Node::TYPE_CONSTANT) {
			ConstantNode *c = static_cast<ConstantNode *>(op->arguments[i]);
			if (c->value.get_type() == Variant::REAL || c->value.get_type() == Variant::INT) {
				constants.push_back(c->value);
				constant = true;
			}
		} else {
			constant = false;
		}
	}
```
But for int variables a larger iterable structure is needed.
